@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/world/dispatcher#> .
@prefix courier: <http://example.org/orexis/courier#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix planning: <http://example.org/orexis/planning#> .

<> a planning:DesireGraph .    # this document is one graph, and this is what it is
<> orexis:beliefsOf :dispatcher .    # and whose it is — said here, never by the file's name

#  ONE DESIRE, and a desire rather than a want: the dispatcher runs for good, asking at every
#  instant that every parcel stand where it is owed.
#
#  THE DELIVERY points at the domain's shape and the domain's estimate, as the courier's want
#  does. The shape targets every parcel, so the derivation mints one want PER PARCEL — two
#  instances in trouble are two wants, as two debts are — unless the world's constraint can make
#  their plans collide, and here it can: both vans reach every cell of the one grid, so the two
#  parcels are ONE want and one search (one-mind-couples-the-wants-a-constraint-can-make-collide).
#
#  THAT NO CELL HOLDS TWO VANS IS NOT A DESIRE and is stated in `constraints.ttl`: it is what the
#  world says is possible, and a world violating it is impossible rather than unwanted. It was a
#  desire here, the aversion `no_cell_holds_two_vans`, and two vans posed on one cell minted a want
#  a drive satisfied — physics repaired as a preference, which is the wrong modality.

:dispatcher planning:holds :every_parcel_delivered .

:every_parcel_delivered a planning:Desire ;
    rdfs:label "every parcel where it is owed" ;
    planning:metWhen courier:delivered ;
    planning:estimates courier:drivesOwed .
